1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,270
In the last video,
we talked about why

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:05,520
the closing process is
important in general.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:08,640
We'll get more specific in
this video as we discuss

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:11,955
why closing is important for
clients and stakeholders.

5
00:00:11,955 --> 00:00:15,645
A formal closing process is
important not only because

6
00:00:15,645 --> 00:00:17,700
improper closing may
leave you on the hook for

7
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:20,175
incomplete contracts
or incomplete scope,

8
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:21,950
but also because you want

9
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:25,090
stakeholders to feel like
their needs were met.

10
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:26,915
As we mentioned earlier,

11
00:00:26,915 --> 00:00:29,615
a project team's relationship
with customers,

12
00:00:29,615 --> 00:00:31,820
users, vendors, and so on

13
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:33,950
can be impacted by loose ends.

14
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:36,364
If relationships are
impacted negatively,

15
00:00:36,364 --> 00:00:38,855
your team's credibility
may be impacted.

16
00:00:38,855 --> 00:00:41,390
Typically, stakeholders
set the goals and scope

17
00:00:41,390 --> 00:00:43,895
of the project alongside
the project manager,

18
00:00:43,895 --> 00:00:45,635
so a good project manager will

19
00:00:45,635 --> 00:00:47,810
always want to make sure
those stakeholders are

20
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:49,460
satisfied with the quality of

21
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:52,060
deliverables and the
end product too.

22
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:54,470
So how can you make
sure that clients and

23
00:00:54,470 --> 00:00:57,440
key stakeholders are happy
with the project closing?

24
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:00,440
First, you'll want to
decide if your project

25
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:02,375
warrants a small closing process

26
00:01:02,375 --> 00:01:04,040
at the end of each milestone

27
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:05,870
or a formal and more

28
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:08,930
comprehensive closing
phase near the very end.

29
00:01:08,930 --> 00:01:10,640
You may even decide to do

30
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:12,740
both the small closing
process at the end of

31
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,665
each milestone and a more
formal closing phase

32
00:01:15,665 --> 00:01:17,105
at the very end.

33
00:01:17,105 --> 00:01:19,505
You'll determine this
by asking yourself

34
00:01:19,505 --> 00:01:21,845
if a particular
milestone is final,

35
00:01:21,845 --> 00:01:24,110
meaning the milestone
will not need to be

36
00:01:24,110 --> 00:01:26,755
readdressed at a later
time in the project.

37
00:01:26,755 --> 00:01:30,410
If so, having a short,
formal closeout will

38
00:01:30,410 --> 00:01:32,000
ensure that everyone is clear on

39
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,495
the outcomes of that
particular milestone.

40
00:01:34,495 --> 00:01:36,530
For example, let's put this in

41
00:01:36,530 --> 00:01:39,815
the context of the Project
Plant Pals website launch.

42
00:01:39,815 --> 00:01:42,890
Launching the website is
an official milestone,

43
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:44,660
so having a formal close out to

44
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:46,930
the website launch
might make sense.

45
00:01:46,930 --> 00:01:48,680
Yes, there will still be

46
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:50,945
ongoing updates and
maintenance to the site,

47
00:01:50,945 --> 00:01:52,610
but it won't be launched again.

48
00:01:52,610 --> 00:01:55,175
The launch is a one-time event.

49
00:01:55,175 --> 00:01:58,625
You need to make sure you're
handing over deliverables,

50
00:01:58,625 --> 00:02:00,735
putting together the
proper documentation,

51
00:02:00,735 --> 00:02:02,320
and alerting all stakeholders

52
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:04,040
that you've reached
that milestone and

53
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:07,385
that that portion of the
project is now closed.

54
00:02:07,385 --> 00:02:08,720
If you decide to conduct

55
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:11,629
closing processes after
each phase or milestone,

56
00:02:11,629 --> 00:02:14,755
here is what that will
look like for your team.

57
00:02:14,755 --> 00:02:17,360
First, you'll ensure
that the project has

58
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:19,025
satisfied the strategic goals

59
00:02:19,025 --> 00:02:20,945
that it was intended to meet.

60
00:02:20,945 --> 00:02:23,360
To begin, you'll want to refer to

61
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:26,090
prior documentation such
as your statement of work,

62
00:02:26,090 --> 00:02:29,885
request for proposal, risk
register, and RACI chart.

63
00:02:29,885 --> 00:02:32,195
You recall that these documents

64
00:02:32,195 --> 00:02:33,950
were discussed in
previous sections,

65
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:37,045
so feel free to revisit
those as a refresher.

66
00:02:37,045 --> 00:02:39,335
While doing so, ask yourself,

67
00:02:39,335 --> 00:02:42,305
was all of the required work
in the elapsed phase done?

68
00:02:42,305 --> 00:02:44,900
Were all identified
issues addressed?

69
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:48,610
Did every team member complete
their assigned tasks?

70
00:02:48,610 --> 00:02:50,900
Then you'll put together closing

71
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:53,825
documentation such as
creating closeout reports,

72
00:02:53,825 --> 00:02:55,940
which we'll cover in depth later.

73
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:57,725
You'll build and review

74
00:02:57,725 --> 00:02:59,630
that documentation
with team members to

75
00:02:59,630 --> 00:03:01,160
make sure that every aspect of

76
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:03,370
the project has been discussed.

77
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:05,440
You'll also review notes from

78
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:08,575
any retrospectives you and
your team participated in.

79
00:03:08,575 --> 00:03:11,050
This way, your team members
will get a chance to

80
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:13,990
speak about the aspects
they liked or didn't like,

81
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:16,385
and they'll leave with
a sense of closure.

82
00:03:16,385 --> 00:03:19,295
Next, you'll conduct
administrative closure

83
00:03:19,295 --> 00:03:20,985
of the procurement process.

84
00:03:20,985 --> 00:03:23,005
Close any contracts necessary,

85
00:03:23,005 --> 00:03:24,775
deliver the payments to vendors,

86
00:03:24,775 --> 00:03:26,680
and retrieve all
final deliverables

87
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:28,220
from contracted workers.

88
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:29,980
This is really important so

89
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:31,450
that external stakeholders and

90
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:33,490
contracted workers
can understand that

91
00:03:33,490 --> 00:03:36,310
the phase or the project
is officially over.

92
00:03:36,310 --> 00:03:38,350
Then you'll want to formally

93
00:03:38,350 --> 00:03:41,255
recognize the completion
of the phase, if necessary.

94
00:03:41,255 --> 00:03:43,510
Make sure all
stakeholders are aware

95
00:03:43,510 --> 00:03:45,995
that a phase or
project is ending.

96
00:03:45,995 --> 00:03:49,010
This may be as simple as
sending out an email

97
00:03:49,010 --> 00:03:50,690
notifying them that
you've achieved

98
00:03:50,690 --> 00:03:53,705
this milestone or may
warrant a larger meeting.

99
00:03:53,705 --> 00:03:57,140
Finally, you'll complete any
necessary follow-up work.

100
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:59,090
This includes things
like gathering

101
00:03:59,090 --> 00:04:01,820
final feedback and
conducting closing surveys.

102
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:04,790
This way, you'll proactively
help stakeholders with

103
00:04:04,790 --> 00:04:08,080
future issues by following
up and offering support.

104
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:11,330
If ,instead of closing
after certain phases or

105
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:13,160
milestones, you've decided to

106
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:15,080
close at the very
end of your project,

107
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:17,320
your process may be a
little bit different.

108
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:19,155
Here is what that
might look like.

109
00:04:19,155 --> 00:04:22,205
First, provide the
necessary training tools,

110
00:04:22,205 --> 00:04:25,280
documentation, and capabilities
to use your product.

111
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:28,370
This includes things like
manuals and how-to guides,

112
00:04:28,370 --> 00:04:29,780
which gives your customers and

113
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:31,610
users an understanding of how to

114
00:04:31,610 --> 00:04:35,155
use your product or service
after the project is closed.

115
00:04:35,155 --> 00:04:37,400
Next, ensure that the project has

116
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,100
satisfied its goals
and desired outcomes.

117
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:42,860
Review the project to make
sure that all tasks and

118
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:46,145
deliverables were completed
and nothing is missing.

119
00:04:46,145 --> 00:04:48,565
Did you accomplish what
you set out to do?

120
00:04:48,565 --> 00:04:50,820
Is the full scope
of work completed?

121
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:53,075
You'll also want to
document acceptance from

122
00:04:53,075 --> 00:04:55,930
all stakeholders like
clients and sponsors.

123
00:04:55,930 --> 00:04:57,560
Ensuring that you have

124
00:04:57,560 --> 00:04:59,120
written proof that
stakeholders are

125
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:00,455
happy with the deliverables and

126
00:05:00,455 --> 00:05:02,495
outcomes is very important.

127
00:05:02,495 --> 00:05:04,350
This could be in retrospectives,

128
00:05:04,350 --> 00:05:05,885
a project completion document,

129
00:05:05,885 --> 00:05:08,260
or any other formal signoff.

130
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:10,250
Then review all contracts and

131
00:05:10,250 --> 00:05:12,580
documentation with
your project team.

132
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:16,220
This includes things
like your SOW, RFP,

133
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:18,395
RACI chart, risk register,

134
00:05:18,395 --> 00:05:21,515
and the procurement documents
that we discussed earlier.

135
00:05:21,515 --> 00:05:24,155
Including your whole team
in this review process

136
00:05:24,155 --> 00:05:26,560
will help you make sure
that nothing is missed.

137
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:28,400
Always document your
lessons learned

138
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:30,410
by conducting a
formal retrospective.

139
00:05:30,410 --> 00:05:33,080
Include your team, any
other teams involved,

140
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:35,915
your stakeholders, and outside
vendors in this meeting.

141
00:05:35,915 --> 00:05:38,905
We'll go over this in more
detail in the next video.

142
00:05:38,905 --> 00:05:43,105
Finally, you can disband
and thank the project team.

143
00:05:43,105 --> 00:05:45,560
The next important
step in closing

144
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:48,380
a project for the stakeholders
is impact reporting.

145
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:50,645
Impact reporting is a
presentation that's

146
00:05:50,645 --> 00:05:53,510
given at the end of a project
for key stakeholders,

147
00:05:53,510 --> 00:05:55,640
which typically includes
the stakeholders you

148
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:58,450
had in the initial
kick-off meeting.

149
00:05:58,450 --> 00:06:01,700
The purpose of impact reporting
is to demonstrate how

150
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:03,320
the project went and discuss

151
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:05,575
the impact of your
product or service.

152
00:06:05,575 --> 00:06:07,685
It's important for
the project manager

153
00:06:07,685 --> 00:06:10,160
because you'll be able to
demonstrate the success of

154
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:12,320
your project on your terms and

155
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:15,745
present the work you did to
add value to the business.

156
00:06:15,745 --> 00:06:18,830
In this presentation, you'll
cover how the project

157
00:06:18,830 --> 00:06:21,820
landed in terms of time,
scope, and budget.

158
00:06:21,820 --> 00:06:24,230
You'll state when the
new service or product

159
00:06:24,230 --> 00:06:27,530
launch and discuss any
available feedback from users,

160
00:06:27,530 --> 00:06:29,000
and you'll explain how

161
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,855
the desired outcomes
were achieved.

162
00:06:31,855 --> 00:06:33,740
Now you know more about how

163
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:35,330
stakeholders and customers can

164
00:06:35,330 --> 00:06:38,480
get a clear understanding
that your project is closed.

165
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:39,800
We've discussed why that's

166
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:42,170
necessary and how
your reputation and

167
00:06:42,170 --> 00:06:44,480
credibility can be
affected if you don't

168
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:47,665
do extensive work to close
the project completely.

169
00:06:47,665 --> 00:06:49,235
In the next video,

170
00:06:49,235 --> 00:06:52,010
we'll discuss how your team
can be impacted by closing

171
00:06:52,010 --> 00:06:56,280
a project properly and
improperly. I'll meet you there.
